          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux, specifically gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), often worsens after meals due to several factors. After eating, the stomach needs to expand to accommodate food, which can increase pressure on the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). If the LES is weak or relaxed, it may not prevent stomach acid from flowing back up into the esophagus, leading to symptoms like heartburn and regurgitation.\n\nCertain types of foods and meal characteristics can further contribute to this issue. For example, fatty meals can slow stomach emptying, which extends the time that reflux can occur. Larger or late meals are also known to provoke reflux symptoms because they may keep the stomach fuller for longer, increasing the pressure on the LES (source: General clinical reference on GERD).\n\nAdditionally, behaviors such as bending forward or lying down after eating can exacerbate reflux symptoms, making it a common experience after meals (source: General clinical reference on GERD). \n\nIf you find that you experience significant discomfort after eating, it may be worth discussing this with a clinician to explore management options.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
